After a day and a half of struggling, I succeeded in compiling (tty,winnt)
nethack using mingw. Since it was done with mingw, the keyboard handler and
IBMGraphics both work, something that wasn't true with my cygwin compile a
few days back. Best of all, it is save- and bones-compatible with vanilla.

The next step was to apply Jukka Lahtinen's patches from
http://www.netsonic.fi/~walker/nethack.html, which also succeeded. My
original motivation was to get a binary-compatible version that
incorporates the HP warning patch; I wanted binary-compatible because I
don't want to give up my bones files, which have some nice stuff in there.

The reason this was so difficult is that the instruction from Install.NT to
just "download the 2.0 release of Mingw" is misleading. Mingw is a very
confusing project - the downloads page has dozens of files.  Once I found
the "Real Dumb Quickstart" at
http://www.mingw.org/MinGWiki/index.php/RealDumbQuickstart on the mingw
wiki, I was halfway there; but it wasn't perfect for my purposes, because
when you're done you have an MSYS environment, with sh, and the
Makefile.GCC assumes that the shell is going to be cmd.exe -- it uses "if
not exist" and "copy" and "del" a lot. And I couldn't just use the unix
Makefile, because it would have tried to link with ncurses or terminfo,
instead of the winnt console driver and the keyboard handler.  sigh ...

Brutally hacking away at the makefile, I got it to the point where I could
compile the nethack.exe binary, which I then dropped into my existing
vanilla playground, and it worked.

If anyone's interested, I will try to finish whipping the makefile into
shape, and I'll write up what I did and put it on my blog.


